 true] [17592186413863 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413849 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413863 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413854 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413863 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413863 1077 "selection" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413863 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413835 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413863 1079 #uuid "fb5f2ce0-c095-5ce3-be9e-db12b1e6fb29" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413863 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413864 1069 "q-66348" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413864 1071 "Given $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $g(x)=2x-1,$ find  $(fg)(3).$" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413864 1072 #uuid "a8d2e394-0754-5534-a3fd-34ae3db4d32c" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413864 1074 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =(3)^{2}+1=10 \\\\ g(3) & =2(3)-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(3)=f(3)g(3)=10\\cdot 5=50\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)(2x-1) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(3) & =2(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}+2(3)-1 \\\\  & =54-9+6-1 \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413862 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413865 1069 "q-66422" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413865 1071 "Given $f(x)=4x-6$ and $g(x)=5x-10,$ find  $(fg)(4).$" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413865 1072 #uuid "e96a222d-7bc2-5df0-a9b1-bdb0a30abe0c" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413865 1074 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(4),$ we find $f(4)$ and $g(4),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(4)$ and $g(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4(4)-6=10 \\\\ g(4) & =5(4)-10=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(4)=f(4)g(4)=10\\cdot 10=100\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(4x-6)(5x-10) \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-70x+60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(4) & =20(4)^{2}-70(4)+60 \\\\  & =320-280+60 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413860 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413866 1069 "q-78620" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413866 1071 "Find  $(f/g)(-1)$  for $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+x.$" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413866 1072 #uuid "fe5aa5dd-14e3-52ea-8c5e-90aad88d589b" 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413866 1074 "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then divide the results. \n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, since the denominator of a fraction can't be zero, we can't divide $f(-1)$ by $g(-1).$\n\nTherefore, the value  $(f/g)(-1)$  is undefined." 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413867 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413871 13194139534202 17592186045417 true] [17592186413867 1062 #uuid "03bc34b4-7201-59a8-8